    model_api_content_id: "e2db12e7ab6141bcb8ee97b40e0619cb"
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "# SMARTMESS AI \342\200\224 FINAL HACKATHON PRODUCT COMPLETION\n\nWe are completing the existing SmartMess AI hackathon application. The latest project has been imported from the updated GitHub repository `Milhaan-7/smartmess-ai`, branch `main`.\n\nThis is an existing project with working code and previous changes. Our deadline is close.\n\n## NON-NEGOTIABLE RULE\n\n**Improve and complete the existing application. Do not rebuild it, reset it, replace its architecture, or discard existing changes.**\n\nRead `AI_CONTEXT.md`, `TASK_STATUS.md`, and `SUBMISSION_GUIDE.md` if present. Inspect the actual current source code before editing. Treat reproducible tests and current code as authoritative rather than relying on earlier claims.\n\nWork in small, reversible stages. Preserve a checkpoint after each stable stage. If quota limits or permission errors interrupt work, stop safely and report the exact status.\n\n# PRIORITY 1 \342\200\224 REDESIGN THE LOGIN EXPERIENCE\n\nThe current login page has three large role cards and the heading \"Select Your Dining Hall Persona.\" It is functional but does not yet create the premium, polished first impression we want for a hackathon product.\n\nRedesign the existing login page while preserving its role-routing and authentication behavior.\n\n### Desired visual direction\n\n- Premium AI SaaS product.\n- Deep navy background, refined emerald-green accents, and accessible high-contrast text.\n- Professional typography, consistent spacing, subtle gradients, and restrained glass or card effects.\n- A clear SmartMess AI brand identity and the subtitle: \"AI-powered institutional dining intelligence.\"\n- A memorable headline focused on smarter food preparation and waste prevention.\n- A compact product preview or visual area that communicates demand forecasting, menu approval, and waste analytics.\n- Subtle motion only if it does not affect performance or accessibility.\n- Fully responsive layout for desktop and mobile.\n\n### Login experience\n\n- Make the first screen look like a real product sign-in page, not just a role chooser.\n- Provide a clear sign-in panel and a compact role selector for Manager, Kitchen Staff, and Student.\n- Use the existing authentication implementation wherever available.\n- If the app uses synthetic demo role selection rather than real authentication, label it honestly as demo sign-in.\n- Preserve the existing three-role routing and sign-out behavior.\n- Show a clear active-role selection and understandable validation/error states.\n- Keep demo entry quick and reliable for a screen-recorded presentation.\n- Never introduce fake credentials or weaken real production authentication.\n- Do not add an unnecessary login service or external authentication dependency.\n\nDo not invent live institutional statistics, actual customer counts, or measured waste-reduction percentages in the login preview. Use clearly labeled illustrative demo data when necessary.\n\n# PRIORITY 2 \342\200\224 BUILD A FULL SMARTMESS AI CONVERSATIONAL ASSISTANT\n\nWe already have a Gemini AI Forecast Insights card on the Forecasts page. That feature is different from the conversational chatbot we now want.\n\n**Add a new, dedicated \"AI Assistant\" feature to the existing application. Keep the current Forecasts page and its Gemini Insights card.**\n\nThe chatbot should answer natural-language questions about SmartMess AI using the application data that the current user is authorized to access.\n\n## 2A. Chat interface\n\n- Add \"AI Assistant\" to the existing navigation without removing current navigation items.\n- Build a polished conversational chat screen consistent with the redesigned login and existing dashboard design.\n- Include message bubbles, timestamps where useful, a message input, send button, loading indicator, retryable error states, and a clear-chat action.\n- Prevent duplicate submissions and handle empty messages.\n- Support reasonable follow-up questions within the current conversation.\n- Preserve conversation history across ordinary UI interactions. Use existing storage patterns; do not introduce unnecessary infrastructure.\n- Include clickable suggested questions.\n\nSuggested prompts:\n1. \"How much food was wasted in the last 30 days?\"\n2. \"Which dishes had the most leftovers?\"\n3. \"Compare this month\'s recorded waste with last month.\"\n4. \"How has lunch attendance changed over time?\"\n5. \"Which meals consistently have excess preparation?\"\n6. \"Summarize recent demand trends.\"\n7. \"What practical steps could reduce leftovers?\"\n8. \"Show me the current approved preparation targets.\"\n\n## 2B. Data-aware questions and answers\n\nInspect the actual data models, API routes, state store, menus, meal records, preparation logs, serving/consumption records, leftover records, forecasts, and waste analytics.\n\nImplement a small set of safe, testable backend analytics/query functions that reuse the existing architecture.\n\nWhere the necessary data exists, support:\n- Total recorded food waste over a requested date range.\n- Comparison of recorded waste between two periods.\n- Dishes with the highest recorded leftovers.\n- Historical attendance or serving trends.\n- Demand forecast summaries and comparisons with historical baselines.\n- Approved preparation targets and relevant operational status.\n- Practical recommendations grounded in the retrieved data.\n\nRequirements:\n- Compute all numerical totals, date ranges, comparisons, and rankings in application code.\n- Pass Gemini only the relevant aggregated results and minimum context required to formulate an answer.\n- Ask for clarification if a date range or question is ambiguous.\n- Clearly distinguish recorded waste, estimated demand, and recommended preparation quantities.\n- Do not calculate actual waste by simply assuming prepared food minus diners served unless the existing data model supports that calculation.\n- If historical records are missing, say so rather than generating fake data.\n- If data is synthetic or held in browser localStorage, disclose that the response uses demo data and identify the scope of that data.\n- Never claim to have queried a live institution or database unless that connection actually exists.\n\n## 2C. Gemini integration\n\nInspect the existing `@google/genai` dependency and backend Gemini integration. Reuse them where appropriate.\n\nImplement a dedicated server-side chat endpoint through the existing Express architecture.\n\n- Read `GEMINI_API_KEY` only from the server environment.\n- Never hardcode or expose the API key in browser code, frontend environment variables, GitHub commits, logs, or user-visible responses.\n- Use a supported model configurable on the server.\n- Handle missing keys, invalid keys, quota errors, network failures, and timeouts gracefully.\n- Do not send the entire database or all historical records to Gemini.\n- Do not let Gemini invent or override the deterministic numerical results.\n- Use validated backend functions or controlled function-calling tools when the model needs to request analytics. The backend must execute and validate those operations.\n- Enforce role permissions on the backend for every data-access operation.\n- Preserve the existing AI Forecast Insights endpoint independently.\n- When Gemini is unavailable, provide a transparent unavailable state. Deterministic analytics summaries may still be displayed if supported by available data, but do not label rule-based responses as Gemini-generated answers.\n- Do not claim live Gemini access unless a real request using a valid key succeeds.\n\nIf a valid key is not configured, finish and test the UI and deterministic analytics first. Document the exact server-side configuration required. Do not ask the user to paste secrets into chat or source code.\n\n# PRIORITY 3 \342\200\224 PRESERVE AND VERIFY THE EXISTING END-TO-END WORKFLOW\n\nKeep the existing three-role ecosystem intact.\n\n## Manager\n- View demand forecasts and historical baselines.\n- Review and approve daily or weekly meal/preparation plans.\n- View menus, waste analytics, and relevant operational records.\n- Use the new AI Assistant for questions about authorized operational data.\n\n## Kitchen Staff\n- View the manager-approved preparation targets.\n- Record actual preparation, serving/consumption, and leftovers using the existing features.\n- Use the AI Assistant only for authorized operational questions.\n- Never show a pending or unapproved plan as an approved instruction.\n\n## Student\n- View published menus, portions, meal timings, and weekly dining information.\n- Receive in-app notifications when the manager approves a daily or weekly menu.\n- Open the corresponding approved menu from the notification.\n- Use the AI Assistant only for student-appropriate information, such as published menus, meal times, and approved dining information.\n\n## Approval-to-notification workflow\n\nVerify this entire sequence:\n1. Manager approves a specific daily or weekly plan.\n2. A notification is created from the actual approval event.\n3. Student sees the correct notification and unread count.\n4. Student opens the corresponding approved menu.\n5. Kitchen Staff sees the same approved preparation plan and targets.\n6. Role switching and page refresh do not erase shared demo records.\n\nDo not generate duplicate notifications on rerenders or repeated reads. Pending drafts must not create approval alerts.\n\nIf the application uses localStorage as a demo fallback, describe it honestly as same-browser/profile persistence rather than real cross-device delivery or push notifications.\n\n# PRIORITY 4 \342\200\224 FORECASTING CREDIBILITY\n\nInspect the actual forecast calculation before modifying it.\n\nDetermine whether it uses a trained machine-learning model, a statistical forecasting method, historical averages, a deterministic formula, or synthetic demo values.\n\n- Keep numerical forecasting calculations deterministic and testable.\n- Where sufficient historical records exist, use a time-aware train/test split and compare the forecast against a simple baseline.\n- Calculate relevant metrics such as MAE, RMSE, and WAPE correctly.\n- Do not invent evaluation results or display unsupported accuracy percentages.\n- Clearly disclose the use of synthetic data.\n- Do not call a formula a trained ML model.\n- Do not destabilize the working application to introduce an unvalidated model.\n\nIf the existing forecast is a baseline or deterministic method, retain it when that is the safest honest option and document the actual method.\n\n# PRIORITY 5 \342\200\224 N8N AUTOMATION STATUS\n\nInspect whether a real n8n instance or webhook is available.\n\nIf an endpoint is already configured and can be tested safely, verify the integration and reuse it where appropriate.\n\nOtherwise:\n- Keep the existing documentation.\n- Clearly label n8n as NOT CONNECTED.\n- Do not simulate a successful webhook or claim that notifications were sent externally.\n- Do not spend the remaining deadline setting up unrelated infrastructure.\n\n# PRIORITY 6 \342\200\224 TESTING, SECURITY, AND FINISHING\n\nAfter implementation, run the available:\n- Production build.\n- TypeScript checks.\n- Lint.\n- Existing and new relevant tests.\n\nAdd focused tests for the chatbot:\n- Manager waste-total query over a known date range.\n- Comparison of two periods using known test records.\n- Dish ranking from known leftover data.\n- Missing or insufficient historical data.\n- Missing/invalid Gemini API key and quota/network failures.\n- Backend role permissions for Manager, Kitchen Staff, and Student.\n- Empty input, repeated submissions, and follow-up messages.\n\nAlso recheck:\n- Login and role routing.\n- Manager approval and student notifications.\n- Kitchen target consistency.\n- Persistence after refresh.\n- Existing menus, dashboards, waste analytics, and Forecasts page.\n\nNever remove existing tests simply to make the suite pass. Fix only verified problems and preserve unrelated work.\n\nUpdate `TASK_STATUS.md` with actual results, files changed, working features, limitations, and remaining configuration requirements.\n\n# FINAL DELIVERY REPORT\n\nWhen finished, provide a concise report covering:\n\n1. Login redesign and role-selection behavior.\n2. Chatbot functionality and supported question types.\n3. Actual data source used by the chatbot.\n4. Whether a real Gemini request succeeded.\n5. Forecasting method and evaluation status.\n6. Student notification and kitchen workflow status.\n7. n8n status.\n8. Production build and test results.\n9. Any remaining blockers.\n\n## EXECUTION STRATEGY\n\nInspect first, then implement incrementally.\n\nRecommended order:\n1. Audit the current workspace and preserve a checkpoint.\n2. Improve the login page.\n3. Implement the chatbot UI and deterministic data-query layer.\n4. Connect the server-side Gemini endpoint if credentials are available.\n5. Test role restrictions and existing approval workflows.\n6. Run the build and relevant tests.\n7. Update documentation.\n\nIf a quota limit or permission error occurs, stop and report the exact status. Do not restart the project or repeat completed work.\n\nOur objective is a stable, credible, demo-ready SmartMess AI product, not a collection of unfinished features. Preserve all current changes."
    items {
      text: "# SMARTMESS AI \342\200\224 FINAL HACKATHON PRODUCT COMPLETION\n\nWe are completing the existing SmartMess AI hackathon application. The latest project has been imported from the updated GitHub repository `Milhaan-7/smartmess-ai`, branch `main`.\n\nThis is an existing project with working code and previous changes. Our deadline is close.\n\n## NON-NEGOTIABLE RULE\n\n**Improve and complete the existing application. Do not rebuild it, reset it, replace its architecture, or discard existing changes.**\n\nRead `AI_CONTEXT.md`, `TASK_STATUS.md`, and `SUBMISSION_GUIDE.md` if present. Inspect the actual current source code before editing. Treat reproducible tests and current code as authoritative rather than relying on earlier claims.\n\nWork in small, reversible stages. Preserve a checkpoint after each stable stage. If quota limits or permission errors interrupt work, stop safely and report the exact status.\n\n# PRIORITY 1 \342\200\224 REDESIGN THE LOGIN EXPERIENCE\n\nThe current login page has three large role cards and the heading \"Select Your Dining Hall Persona.\" It is functional but does not yet create the premium, polished first impression we want for a hackathon product.\n\nRedesign the existing login page while preserving its role-routing and authentication behavior.\n\n### Desired visual direction\n\n- Premium AI SaaS product.\n- Deep navy background, refined emerald-green accents, and accessible high-contrast text.\n- Professional typography, consistent spacing, subtle gradients, and restrained glass or card effects.\n- A clear SmartMess AI brand identity and the subtitle: \"AI-powered institutional dining intelligence.\"\n- A memorable headline focused on smarter food preparation and waste prevention.\n- A compact product preview or visual area that communicates demand forecasting, menu approval, and waste analytics.\n- Subtle motion only if it does not affect performance or accessibility.\n- Fully responsive layout for desktop and mobile.\n\n### Login experience\n\n- Make the first screen look like a real product sign-in page, not just a role chooser.\n- Provide a clear sign-in panel and a compact role selector for Manager, Kitchen Staff, and Student.\n- Use the existing authentication implementation wherever available.\n- If the app uses synthetic demo role selection rather than real authentication, label it honestly as demo sign-in.\n- Preserve the existing three-role routing and sign-out behavior.\n- Show a clear active-role selection and understandable validation/error states.\n- Keep demo entry quick and reliable for a screen-recorded presentation.\n- Never introduce fake credentials or weaken real production authentication.\n- Do not add an unnecessary login service or external authentication dependency.\n\nDo not invent live institutional statistics, actual customer counts, or measured waste-reduction percentages in the login preview. Use clearly labeled illustrative demo data when necessary.\n\n# PRIORITY 2 \342\200\224 BUILD A FULL SMARTMESS AI CONVERSATIONAL ASSISTANT\n\nWe already have a Gemini AI Forecast Insights card on the Forecasts page. That feature is different from the conversational chatbot we now want.\n\n**Add a new, dedicated \"AI Assistant\" feature to the existing application. Keep the current Forecasts page and its Gemini Insights card.**\n\nThe chatbot should answer natural-language questions about SmartMess AI using the application data that the current user is authorized to access.\n\n## 2A. Chat interface\n\n- Add \"AI Assistant\" to the existing navigation without removing current navigation items.\n- Build a polished conversational chat screen consistent with the redesigned login and existing dashboard design.\n- Include message bubbles, timestamps where useful, a message input, send button, loading indicator, retryable error states, and a clear-chat action.\n- Prevent duplicate submissions and handle empty messages.\n- Support reasonable follow-up questions within the current conversation.\n- Preserve conversation history across ordinary UI interactions. Use existing storage patterns; do not introduce unnecessary infrastructure.\n- Include clickable suggested questions.\n\nSuggested prompts:\n1. \"How much food was wasted in the last 30 days?\"\n2. \"Which dishes had the most leftovers?\"\n3. \"Compare this month\'s recorded waste with last month.\"\n4. \"How has lunch attendance changed over time?\"\n5. \"Which meals consistently have excess preparation?\"\n6. \"Summarize recent demand trends.\"\n7. \"What practical steps could reduce leftovers?\"\n8. \"Show me the current approved preparation targets.\"\n\n## 2B. Data-aware questions and answers\n\nInspect the actual data models, API routes, state store, menus, meal records, preparation logs, serving/consumption records, leftover records, forecasts, and waste analytics.\n\nImplement a small set of safe, testable backend analytics/query functions that reuse the existing architecture.\n\nWhere the necessary data exists, support:\n- Total recorded food waste over a requested date range.\n- Comparison of recorded waste between two periods.\n- Dishes with the highest recorded leftovers.\n- Historical attendance or serving trends.\n- Demand forecast summaries and comparisons with historical baselines.\n- Approved preparation targets and relevant operational status.\n- Practical recommendations grounded in the retrieved data.\n\nRequirements:\n- Compute all numerical totals, date ranges, comparisons, and rankings in application code.\n- Pass Gemini only the relevant aggregated results and minimum context required to formulate an answer.\n- Ask for clarification if a date range or question is ambiguous.\n- Clearly distinguish recorded waste, estimated demand, and recommended preparation quantities.\n- Do not calculate actual waste by simply assuming prepared food minus diners served unless the existing data model supports that calculation.\n- If historical records are missing, say so rather than generating fake data.\n- If data is synthetic or held in browser localStorage, disclose that the response uses demo data and identify the scope of that data.\n- Never claim to have queried a live institution or database unless that connection actually exists.\n\n## 2C. Gemini integration\n\nInspect the existing `@google/genai` dependency and backend Gemini integration. Reuse them where appropriate.\n\nImplement a dedicated server-side chat endpoint through the existing Express architecture.\n\n- Read `GEMINI_API_KEY` only from the server environment.\n- Never hardcode or expose the API key in browser code, frontend environment variables, GitHub commits, logs, or user-visible responses.\n- Use a supported model configurable on the server.\n- Handle missing keys, invalid keys, quota errors, network failures, and timeouts gracefully.\n- Do not send the entire database or all historical records to Gemini.\n- Do not let Gemini invent or override the deterministic numerical results.\n- Use validated backend functions or controlled function-calling tools when the model needs to request analytics. The backend must execute and validate those operations.\n- Enforce role permissions on the backend for every data-access operation.\n- Preserve the existing AI Forecast Insights endpoint independently.\n- When Gemini is unavailable, provide a transparent unavailable state. Deterministic analytics summaries may still be displayed if supported by available data, but do not label rule-based responses as Gemini-generated answers.\n- Do not claim live Gemini access unless a real request using a valid key succeeds.\n\nIf a valid key is not configured, finish and test the UI and deterministic analytics first. Document the exact server-side configuration required. Do not ask the user to paste secrets into chat or source code.\n\n# PRIORITY 3 \342\200\224 PRESERVE AND VERIFY THE EXISTING END-TO-END WORKFLOW\n\nKeep the existing three-role ecosystem intact.\n\n## Manager\n- View demand forecasts and historical baselines.\n- Review and approve daily or weekly meal/preparation plans.\n- View menus, waste analytics, and relevant operational records.\n- Use the new AI Assistant for questions about authorized operational data.\n\n## Kitchen Staff\n- View the manager-approved preparation targets.\n- Record actual preparation, serving/consumption, and leftovers using the existing features.\n- Use the AI Assistant only for authorized operational questions.\n- Never show a pending or unapproved plan as an approved instruction.\n\n## Student\n- View published menus, portions, meal timings, and weekly dining information.\n- Receive in-app notifications when the manager approves a daily or weekly menu.\n- Open the corresponding approved menu from the notification.\n- Use the AI Assistant only for student-appropriate information, such as published menus, meal times, and approved dining information.\n\n## Approval-to-notification workflow\n\nVerify this entire sequence:\n1. Manager approves a specific daily or weekly plan.\n2. A notification is created from the actual approval event.\n3. Student sees the correct notification and unread count.\n4. Student opens the corresponding approved menu.\n5. Kitchen Staff sees the same approved preparation plan and targets.\n6. Role switching and page refresh do not erase shared demo records.\n\nDo not generate duplicate notifications on rerenders or repeated reads. Pending drafts must not create approval alerts.\n\nIf the application uses localStorage as a demo fallback, describe it honestly as same-browser/profile persistence rather than real cross-device delivery or push notifications.\n\n# PRIORITY 4 \342\200\224 FORECASTING CREDIBILITY\n\nInspect the actual forecast calculation before modifying it.\n\nDetermine whether it uses a trained machine-learning model, a statistical forecasting method, historical averages, a deterministic formula, or synthetic demo values.\n\n- Keep numerical forecasting calculations deterministic and testable.\n- Where sufficient historical records exist, use a time-aware train/test split and compare the forecast against a simple baseline.\n- Calculate relevant metrics such as MAE, RMSE, and WAPE correctly.\n- Do not invent evaluation results or display unsupported accuracy percentages.\n- Clearly disclose the use of synthetic data.\n- Do not call a formula a trained ML model.\n- Do not destabilize the working application to introduce an unvalidated model.\n\nIf the existing forecast is a baseline or deterministic method, retain it when that is the safest honest option and document the actual method.\n\n# PRIORITY 5 \342\200\224 N8N AUTOMATION STATUS\n\nInspect whether a real n8n instance or webhook is available.\n\nIf an endpoint is already configured and can be tested safely, verify the integration and reuse it where appropriate.\n\nOtherwise:\n- Keep the existing documentation.\n- Clearly label n8n as NOT CONNECTED.\n- Do not simulate a successful webhook or claim that notifications were sent externally.\n- Do not spend the remaining deadline setting up unrelated infrastructure.\n\n# PRIORITY 6 \342\200\224 TESTING, SECURITY, AND FINISHING\n\nAfter implementation, run the available:\n- Production build.\n- TypeScript checks.\n- Lint.\n- Existing and new relevant tests.\n\nAdd focused tests for the chatbot:\n- Manager waste-total query over a known date range.\n- Comparison of two periods using known test records.\n- Dish ranking from known leftover data.\n- Missing or insufficient historical data.\n- Missing/invalid Gemini API key and quota/network failures.\n- Backend role permissions for Manager, Kitchen Staff, and Student.\n- Empty input, repeated submissions, and follow-up messages.\n\nAlso recheck:\n- Login and role routing.\n- Manager approval and student notifications.\n- Kitchen target consistency.\n- Persistence after refresh.\n- Existing menus, dashboards, waste analytics, and Forecasts page.\n\nNever remove existing tests simply to make the suite pass. Fix only verified problems and preserve unrelated work.\n\nUpdate `TASK_STATUS.md` with actual results, files changed, working features, limitations, and remaining configuration requirements.\n\n# FINAL DELIVERY REPORT\n\nWhen finished, provide a concise report covering:\n\n1. Login redesign and role-selection behavior.\n2. Chatbot functionality and supported question types.\n3. Actual data source used by the chatbot.\n4. Whether a real Gemini request succeeded.\n5. Forecasting method and evaluation status.\n6. Student notification and kitchen workflow status.\n7. n8n status.\n8. Production build and test results.\n9. Any remaining blockers.\n\n## EXECUTION STRATEGY\n\nInspect first, then implement incrementally.\n\nRecommended order:\n1. Audit the current workspace and preserve a checkpoint.\n2. Improve the login page.\n3. Implement the chatbot UI and deterministic data-query layer.\n4. Connect the server-side Gemini endpoint if credentials are available.\n5. Test role restrictions and existing approval workflows.\n6. Run the build and relevant tests.\n7. Update documentation.\n\nIf a quota limit or permission error occurs, stop and report the exact status. Do not restart the project or repeat completed work.\n\nOur objective is a stable, credible, demo-ready SmartMess AI product, not a collection of unfinished features. Preserve all current changes."
