steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1792552351
      nanos: 985669001
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
    source_trajectory_step_info {
      trajectory_id: "080e128d-a2c7-4b30-b2c1-ba9ecac7f8a1"
      step_index: 330
      metadata_index: 70
      cascade_id: "080e128d-a2c7-4b30-b2c1-ba9ecac7f8a1"
    }
    model_api_content_id: "0b3767e05d604f77a964c071baaabede"
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "# SmartMess AI \342\200\224 Art-Directed Login Redesign (Remove the AI-Generated Template Look)\n\nRedesign the ACTUAL initial login/demo-entry page of my existing SmartMess AI application. The current page looks like a generic AI-generated landing page. I want a deliberate, professionally art-directed enterprise product experience.\n\nDo not create a new project. Do not rebuild the entire application. Do not change backend functionality, role permissions, routing, or existing workflows. Focus on the initial page and its styling.\n\n## 1. Design objective\n\nMake the page feel like a real enterprise software product designed by an experienced product designer for university dining operations.\n\nVisual references in spirit:\n- Editorial typography and restrained layouts.\n- High-quality hospitality and institutional dining photography.\n- Enterprise product onboarding.\n- Strong alignment and generous but purposeful whitespace.\n- A clear, practical path into the demo.\n\nThe design must feel credible, calm, precise, and useful. Do not make it look like an AI startup landing-page template.\n\n## 2. Remove these visual patterns from the current page\n\n- Oversized gradient text spanning multiple lines.\n- Multiple glowing gradients over the background photograph.\n- Huge rounded cards repeated for every user role.\n- Long explanatory paragraphs and three-item checklists inside each role card.\n- Excessive pill badges, decorative icons, borders, and shadows.\n- Generic logo tiles containing a single letter as a substitute for proper brand identity.\n- Repeated \342\200\234AI-powered\342\200\235 labels and redundant product messaging.\n- Marketing content that pushes the role-selection controls below the first screen.\n- Unnecessary animation and neon effects.\n\nDo not simply change the colours and retain the same layout. Rethink the composition.\n\n## 3. New layout: compact editorial split screen\n\nUse a clean, balanced two-column layout with a restrained visual style.\n\n### Left side \342\200\224 approximately 52\342\200\22355%\n\nCreate a visually grounded brand/storytelling panel.\n\n- Use one high-quality, realistic photograph of an institutional dining hall, a clean commercial kitchen, or a well-organized meal-preparation area.\n- Use a natural photographic treatment with restrained darkening only where required for legibility.\n- Avoid strong blur, artificial glowing outlines, neon waves, decorative orbital graphics, and multiple overlapping overlays.\n- Place the SmartMess AI wordmark at the top using a simple leaf or food-related mark from the existing icon library.\n- Use a concise eyebrow label: \342\200\234DINING OPERATIONS INTELLIGENCE\342\200\235.\n- Use this headline: \342\200\234Plan better. Prepare smarter. Waste less.\342\200\235\n- Supporting copy: \342\200\234One workspace for meal demand forecasting, kitchen preparation, and food-waste monitoring.\342\200\235\n- At the bottom, include three compact capabilities in a single row or a simple vertical list:\n  \342\200\234Demand forecasting\342\200\235, \342\200\234Preparation planning\342\200\235, \342\200\234Waste monitoring\342\200\235.\n\nThe left panel should communicate the product in seconds, without looking like an advertisement. Keep the content visually balanced and avoid oversized typography.\n\n### Right side \342\200\224 approximately 45\342\200\22348%\n\nThis is the primary interaction area. Users need to enter the demo, not read a sales pitch.\n\nUse a clean, solid background with ample whitespace.\n\nAt the top of the interaction area, show:\n\n**Welcome to SmartMess AI**\n\n\342\200\234Choose your workspace to continue.\342\200\235\n\nThen show the three demo roles as compact, well-designed selectable rows, not large marketing cards.\n\nEach row should contain:\n- A small, consistent icon.\n- Role name.\n- One concise description.\n- A subtle arrow or clear selection indicator.\n\nRoles and descriptions:\n\n1. **Mess Manager** \342\200\224 Forecasts, menu approvals and waste analytics.\n2. **Kitchen Staff** \342\200\224 Preparation targets and serving records.\n3. **Student / Diner** \342\200\224 Approved menus and dining notifications.\n\nMake the entire row clickable, with a clear hover and keyboard-focus state. Maintain good contrast and predictable spacing.\n\nSelecting a role must use the existing demo sign-in flow. Do not invent email/password authentication, accounts, or forms that the application does not support.\n\nOnly the Mess Manager role should be shown as having access to the AI Assistant. Do not advertise the chatbot to students or kitchen staff.\n\nAt the bottom, include a small, unobtrusive note: \342\200\234Demo environment \302\267 Synthetic data\342\200\235.\n\n## 4. Colour, typography and spacing\n\nUse a restrained, intentional palette:\n- Primary background: warm off-white or very light neutral.\n- Text: deep charcoal or deep forest green.\n- Accent: restrained emerald.\n- Secondary surfaces: subtle neutral tones.\n- Borders: thin and low contrast.\n\nA dark forest-green photographic panel may be used on the left, but avoid turning the whole page into a generic dark-neon interface.\n\nUse the project\'s existing typography if it is suitable. Otherwise, choose a clean, highly legible sans-serif with a deliberate hierarchy:\n- Moderate headline size.\n- Clear section heading.\n- Compact descriptions.\n- Consistent body text.\n- No unnecessary uppercase labels everywhere.\n\nUse a consistent spacing scale and align content to a clear grid. Prefer flat surfaces and subtle dividers over nested cards and heavy shadows.\n\n## 5. First-screen usability\n\nAt a typical laptop resolution, the user should see:\n- The brand and complete headline.\n- The concise product explanation.\n- All three role options.\n- The synthetic-data disclosure.\n\nAvoid forcing the user to scroll merely to choose a role. If the viewport is too short, allow sensible scrolling rather than clipping content.\n\nOn mobile:\n- Stack the storytelling and interaction areas.\n- Reduce image height.\n- Keep all three role options easy to find and tap.\n- Prevent horizontal scrolling.\n- Maintain readable text and accessible focus states.\n\n## 6. Preserve existing functionality\n\nBefore editing, inspect the current login component and existing role-selection flow.\n\nPreserve:\n- The existing three demo roles.\n- Correct role-specific navigation.\n- Manager-only AI Assistant restrictions in both frontend and backend.\n- Existing routing and session/token behaviour.\n- All dashboards, forecasting, menu management, kitchen operations, approvals, waste analytics, and notifications.\n- Existing server and API logic.\n\nDo not edit unrelated pages unless required to fix a regression caused by the login redesign.\n\nDo not introduce new dependencies just for visual styling if the existing project can achieve the result.\n\n## 7. Validate the actual result\n\nAfter implementing the redesign:\n\n1. Run the production build and relevant tests.\n2. Launch the actual application preview.\n3. Inspect the rendered initial page at desktop and mobile widths.\n4. Confirm that the oversized gradient headline, repetitive large role cards, and heavy overlays are gone.\n5. Confirm that all three role options are visible and functional.\n6. Click each role and verify correct navigation.\n7. Check for broken images, clipping, overflow, console errors, and broken interactions.\n8. Correct the rendered UI if it still looks like a generic AI-generated template.\n\nDo not stop after editing the JSX or CSS. Do not report success based on a successful build alone.\n\nAt the end, briefly explain the changes and report whether the actual preview was inspected.\n\nStart by inspecting the existing login component, then implement this new art direction directly in the existing page."
    items {
      text: "# SmartMess AI \342\200\224 Art-Directed Login Redesign (Remove the AI-Generated Template Look)\n\nRedesign the ACTUAL initial login/demo-entry page of my existing SmartMess AI application. The current page looks like a generic AI-generated landing page. I want a deliberate, professionally art-directed enterprise product experience.\n\nDo not create a new project. Do not rebuild the entire application. Do not change backend functionality, role permissions, routing, or existing workflows. Focus on the initial page and its styling.\n\n## 1. Design objective\n\nMake the page feel like a real enterprise software product designed by an experienced product designer for university dining operations.\n\nVisual references in spirit:\n- Editorial typography and restrained layouts.\n- High-quality hospitality and institutional dining photography.\n- Enterprise product onboarding.\n- Strong alignment and generous but purposeful whitespace.\n- A clear, practical path into the demo.\n\nThe design must feel credible, calm, precise, and useful. Do not make it look like an AI startup landing-page template.\n\n## 2. Remove these visual patterns from the current page\n\n- Oversized gradient text spanning multiple lines.\n- Multiple glowing gradients over the background photograph.\n- Huge rounded cards repeated for every user role.\n- Long explanatory paragraphs and three-item checklists inside each role card.\n- Excessive pill badges, decorative icons, borders, and shadows.\n- Generic logo tiles containing a single letter as a substitute for proper brand identity.\n- Repeated \342\200\234AI-powered\342\200\235 labels and redundant product messaging.\n- Marketing content that pushes the role-selection controls below the first screen.\n- Unnecessary animation and neon effects.\n\nDo not simply change the colours and retain the same layout. Rethink the composition.\n\n## 3. New layout: compact editorial split screen\n\nUse a clean, balanced two-column layout with a restrain