Alignment, whitespace, overflow, and scrolling.\n- Loading, empty, error, and success states.\n- Keyboard navigation, focus indicators, contrast, and touch targets.\n- Responsive behaviour at desktop, tablet, and mobile widths.\n\nRemove redundant decoration and visual clutter. Do not add fake data, meaningless charts, fake testimonials, fake performance statistics, or buttons that do nothing.\n\n### Login page \342\200\224 Design A\n\nVerify that the actual initial route shows the requested split-screen experience:\n- Left: SmartMess AI branding, institutional dining imagery, product messaging, and three feature highlights.\n- Right: welcome panel and clear demo-role selection for Mess Manager, Kitchen Staff, and Student/Diner.\n- Responsive desktop split layout and mobile stacking.\n- Clear disclosure that demo access uses synthetic data.\n\nIf Design A is already correct, preserve it. If it is incomplete or the old layout still appears, fix the actual login route and inspect the rendered preview again.\n\n## PRIORITY 2 \342\200\224 Audit each role independently\n\n### A. Mess Manager\n\nVerify:\n- Manager dashboard and summary metrics.\n- Demand forecasts and preparation recommendations.\n- Menu planning and management.\n- Plan review and approval.\n- Waste and leftover analytics.\n- Relevant operational trends.\n- Manager-only AI Assistant.\n- Useful error, loading, and empty states.\n\nEnsure metrics correspond to the records and filters shown. Clearly distinguish forecasts, estimates, approved quantities, historical observations, and synthetic demo values.\n\nNever invent financial figures or claim actual cost savings or waste reduction without evidence.\n\n### B. Kitchen Staff\n\nVerify:\n- Correct role-specific dashboard and navigation.\n- Visibility of approved preparation targets.\n- Correct menu, meal-period, and preparation information.\n- Existing preparation and serving logs.\n- Operational feedback after successful or failed actions.\n- Updates following manager approval.\n\nKitchen Staff must not access the manager-only AI Assistant or private institutional analytics.\n\n### C. Student/Diner\n\nVerify:\n- Approved menu visibility.\n- Meal timings and dining information.\n- Relevant notifications and unread counts.\n- Correct notification navigation.\n- Clear empty states when a menu is unavailable.\n- Responsive layout.\n\nStudents must not access manager-only chat, institutional profit/loss, other students\' private records, or internal kitchen analytics.\n\n## PRIORITY 3 \342\200\224 Verify authorization and security\n\nThe latest changes reportedly restrict the AI Assistant to Mess Manager through the sidebar, frontend routes, and backend authorization.\n\nVerify all three layers rather than assuming they work.\n\n1. Mess Manager can access the AI Assistant.\n2. Kitchen Staff cannot access it through the UI or a direct route.\n3. Student/Diner cannot access it through the UI or a direct route.\n4. Direct requests to `POST /api/v1/chat` from unauthorized roles receive HTTP `403` with the expected JSON error.\n5. The backend validates the existing trusted session, token, or supported demo-authentication mechanism.\n6. A frontend-supplied role string alone cannot grant manager permissions.\n7. Other API endpoints do not unintentionally expose manager-only financial or operational data.\n\nPreserve the existing demo-login mechanism. Do not falsely describe demo authentication as production-grade identity verification.\n\nAdd or update targeted tests if any of these checks fail. Do not remove tests or weaken assertions.\n\n## PRIORITY 4 \342\200\224 Verify AI Assistant and Gemini integration\n\nInspect the existing AI Assistant implementation, API client, Express route, authentication middleware, and server configuration.\n\nThe previous HTML/JSON error was:\n\n`Unexpected token \'<\', \"<!doctype \"... is not valid JSON`\n\nThe frontend and backend reportedly have improved response handling. Confirm that the actual application no longer experiences this failure.\n\nTest:\n- Correct request URL and HTTP method.\n- Successful JSON responses with the expected content type.\n- Non-2xx responses.\n- HTML responses and malformed JSON.\n- Missing or invalid Gemini API key.\n- Quota and network failures.\n- Rule-based fallback behaviour.\n- Manager-only authorization.\n\n### Data-grounded answers\n\nTest questions such as:\n- \342\200\234How much idli should be prepared for tomorrow\'s breakfast?\342\200\235\n- \342\200\234Which dishes had the most leftovers this week?\342\200\235\n- \342\200\234How does attendance compare with the previous period?\342\200\235\n- \342\200\234Which preparation targets have been approved?\342\200\235\n- \342\200\234What actions could reduce food waste?\342\200\235\n\nFor preparation recommendations:\n1. Identify the applicable date and meal period.\n2. Check the available menu.\n3. Retrieve the approved preparation target if one exists.\n4. Otherwise calculate an estimate only if the available records support it.\n5. State the quantity, unit, and basis.\n6. Clearly distinguish an estimate from an approved target.\n7. Report insufficient data instead of inventing an answer.\n\nKeep operational calculations grounded in actual application records. Gemini can explain verified results, but must not fabricate records or numerical outcomes.\n\n### API key safety\n\nRead `GEMINI_API_KEY` only from the server-side environment. Never expose it in frontend code, responses, logs, prompts, screenshots, or GitHub.\n\nIf possible, make a real Gemini API request and report whether it succeeds. If the key is unavailable, invalid, or quota-limited, preserve the fallback and state the actual outcome.\n\nDo not enable billing or create cloud resources without explicit approval. Do not claim live Gemini integration works unless a real request succeeds.\n\n## PRIORITY 5 \342\200\224 Verify the complete business workflow\n\nTest the following sequence in the running application:\n\n1. Manager creates or updates a preparation plan.\n2. Manager approves the plan.\n3. The approved target becomes available to Kitchen Staff.\n4. Student/Diner receives the intended notification.\n5. The notification unread count updates correctly.\n6. Opening the notification leads to the appropriate approved menu or screen.\n\nVerify that the UI reflects the underlying application state. Check for stale data, duplicate notifications, broken navigation, and inconsistent role-specific views.\n\nDetermine the actual persistence model. If state is stored only in browser localStorage, say so. Do not claim cross-device synchronization or database persistence without verifying it.\n\n## PRIORITY 6 \342\200\224 Forecasting, analytics, and integration credibility\n\nInspect the current forecasting implementation.\n\nVerify:\n- Historical baselines and trend calculations.\n- Date and meal-period handling.\n- Units and preparation quantities.\n- Safety buffers and rounding.\n- Forecast versus actual comparisons.\n- Waste and leftover calculations.\n- Filters, charts, and summary cards.\n\nEnsure the UI accurately describes the model. If forecasting is deterministic or rule-based, label it accordingly rather than claiming a trained machine-learning model.\n\nDo not fabricate:\n- Profit or loss.\n- Revenue or operating costs.\n- Food-waste reductions.\n- Forecast accuracy or model evaluation metrics.\n- Real institution data or operational results.\n\nCheck whether PostgreSQL/Cloud SQL, n8n, external notifications, and other integrations are genuinely connected. Keep unconfigured integrations clearly labelled as such.\n\n## PRIORITY 7 \342\200\224 Complete visual and interaction polish\n\nReview every page and fix genuine defects, including:\n- Broken image assets or icons.\n- Inconsistent button variants.\n- Misaligned headers and cards.\n- Unreadable chart labels.\n- Tables overflowing small screens.\n- Overlapping dialogs or dropdowns.\n- Missing loading indicators.\n- Dead buttons and nonfunctional links.\n- Misleading empty states.\n- Console errors and failed network requests.\n\nUse existing dependencies and components where practical. Avoid introducing unnecessary libraries, major architectural changes, or risky visual rewrites.\n\nDo not sacrifice usability for decoration. Prioritize clarity, consistent spacing, responsive layouts, and working interactions.\n\n## PRIORITY 8 \342\200\224 Testing and validation\n\nRun the available:\n- TypeScript checks.\n- Production build.\n- Unit tests.\n- Relevant integration tests.\n- API and authorization tests.\n- Forecasting and fallback tests.\n\nThen inspect the actual browser preview.\n\nVerify:\n1. Design A appears on the actual initial route.\n2. Desktop and mobile layouts render correctly.\n3. All three demo roles navigate to the correct experiences.\n4. The AI Assistant is accessible only to Mess Manager.\n5. Backend authorization rejects unauthorized roles.\n6. Manager chat returns valid JSON.\n7. A preparation question uses available records or reports missing data.\n8. Gemini succeeds or fallback behaviour is honestly identified.\n9. Manager approval updates kitchen targets and student notifications.\n10. Existing dashboards, menus, forecasts, and analytics remain functional.\n11. No new runtime errors, broken routes, or failed API requests remain.\n\nIf the application reports runtime errors, inspect their exact details and fix the real cause. A successful build is not sufficient proof of runtime correctness.\n\nIf browser preview, live Gemini calls, or external integrations cannot be tested, explicitly report what remains unverified.\n\n## PRIORITY 9 \342\200\224 Final submission readiness\n\nReview the existing project documentation and submission materials without overwriting useful work.\n\nEnsure:\n- Setup and run instructions match the current project.\n- Demo roles and synthetic data are clearly disclosed.\n- Implemented features are distinguished from planned or unconfigured integrations.\n- Architecture and forecasting descriptions match the actual code.\n- Known limitations are documented.\n- Existing tests and source files remain intact.\n- The project is ready for a stable demonstration.\n\nDo not push commits, publish deployments, or change repository settings unless explicitly authorized.\n\n## FINAL REPORT\n\nProvide a concise, evidence-based report containing:\n\n1. Files changed and the reason for each change.\n2. UI defects found and fixed.\n3. Actual login and dashboard preview verification.\n4. Manager, Kitchen Staff, and Student/Diner test results.\n5. AI Assistant authorization results.\n6. API root cause and verification results.\n7. Gemini live-request or fallback status.\n8. Approval-to-notification workflow results.\n9. Build and test results, including failures or skipped tests.\n10. Actual persistence and integration limitations.\n11. Remaining issues that could affect the hackathon demo.\n\nDo not claim that every feature works merely because the build passes. Distinguish code changes from features genuinely tested in the running application.\n\nStart by inspecting the current state, identifying the highest-impact verified gaps, and fixing them in a safe order. Preserve working features and avoid unnecessary rewrites."
    }
  }
}
