    user_requests: "Fix the errors in the app\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_START]\nfix_it_trigger: manual\nerror 0: [DB] Database configuration error: Missing required environment variables (SQL_HOST, SQL_DB_NAME, SQL_USER, SQL_PASSWORD). Configure these in .env before using database persistence.\nerror 1: [Server] Seed check deferred or non-fatal: Failed query: select \"id\", \"name\", \"location\", \"created_at\", \"updated_at\" from \"institutions\" where \"institutions\".\"id\" = $1\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_END]"
    user_requests: "# SMARTMESS AI \342\200\224 FINAL APPLICATION AUDIT, UI POLISH & HACKATHON READINESS\n\nYou are continuing work on my existing SmartMess AI project. The latest implementation reportedly includes the Design A login page, manager-only AI Assistant access, server-side role authorization, improved JSON handling, and AI fallback behaviour.\n\nYour task is to conduct a comprehensive final audit of the ENTIRE application, fix remaining real issues, improve visual consistency, and verify the running app before declaring completion.\n\n## NON-NEGOTIABLE RULES\n\n1. Do not create a new project or replace the current application.\n2. Do not reset the repository or discard working changes.\n3. Inspect the current code and rendered application before editing.\n4. Preserve all working functionality, existing API contracts, routes, and tests.\n5. Do not rewrite components merely to demonstrate activity.\n6. Fix verified problems first; make visual improvements only where they add real value.\n7. Never claim a feature is verified unless it was actually tested.\n8. Do not expose API keys, secrets, private records, or sensitive server errors.\n9. If the environment cannot perform a test, report that limitation honestly.\n\n# PRIORITY 1 \342\200\224 Audit the actual UI across the entire application\n\nInspect the actual rendered application, including every existing page and dashboard used by each role.\n\n### Global design system\n\nMaintain a consistent visual identity:\n- Deep navy: `#07111F`\n- Elevated panels: `#0D1B2A`\n- Emerald accent: `#10D9A0`\n- High-contrast white primary text.\n- Muted blue-grey secondary text.\n- Consistent typography, spacing, radii, borders, shadows, icons, and button styles.\n\nMake the product look like a cohesive, premium AI SaaS platform, not a collection of unrelated templates.\n\nAudit:\n- Navigation and active states.\n- Page headings and content hierarchy.\n- Cards, tables, charts, forms, dialogs, dropdowns, and notifications.\n- Alignment, whitespace, overflow, and scrolling.\n- Loading, empty, error, and success states.\n- Keyboard navigation, focus indicators, contrast, and touch targets.\n- Responsive behaviour at desktop, tablet, and mobile widths.\n\nRemove redundant decoration and visual clutter. Do not add fake data, meaningless charts, fake testimonials, fake performance statistics, or buttons that do nothing.\n\n### Login page \342\200\224 Design A\n\nVerify that the actual initial route shows the requested split-screen experience:\n- Left: SmartMess AI branding, institutional dining imagery, product messaging, and three feature highlights.\n- Right: welcome panel and clear demo-role selection for Mess Manager, Kitchen Staff, and Student/Diner.\n- Responsive desktop split layout and mobile stacking.\n- Clear disclosure that demo access uses synthetic data.\n\nIf Design A is already correct, preserve it. If it is incomplete or the old layout still appears, fix the actual login route and inspect the rendered preview again.\n\n# PRIORITY 2 \342\200\224 Audit each role independently\n\n## A. Mess Manager\n\nVerify:\n- Manager dashboard and summary metrics.\n- Demand forecasts and preparation recommendations.\n- Menu planning and management.\n- Plan review and approval.\n- Waste and leftover analytics.\n- Relevant operational trends.\n- Manager-only AI Assistant.\n- Useful error, loading, and empty states.\n\nEnsure metrics correspond to the records and filters shown. Clearly distinguish forecasts, estimates, approved quantities, historical observations, and synthetic demo values.\n\nNever invent financial figures or claim actual cost savings or waste reduction without evidence.\n\n## B. Kitchen Staff\n\nVerify:\n- Correct role-specific dashboard and navigation.\n- Visibility of approved preparation targets.\n- Correct menu, meal-period, and preparation information.\n- Existing preparation and serving logs.\n- Operational feedback after successful or failed actions.\n- Updates following manager approval.\n\nKitchen Staff must not access the manager-only AI Assistant or private institutional analytics.\n\n## C. Student/Diner\n\nVerify:\n- Approved menu visibility.\n- Meal timings and dining information.\n- Relevant notifications and unread counts.\n- Correct notification navigation.\n- Clear empty states when a menu is unavailable.\n- Responsive layout.\n\nStudents must not access manager-only chat, institutional profit/loss, other students\' private records, or internal kitchen analytics.\n\n# PRIORITY 3 \342\200\224 Verify authorization and security\n\nThe latest changes reportedly restrict the AI Assistant to Mess Manager through the sidebar, frontend routes, and backend authorization.\n\nVerify all three layers rather than assuming they work.\n\n1. Mess Manager can access the AI Assistant.\n2. Kitchen Staff cannot access it through the UI or a direct route.\n3. Student/Diner cannot access it through the UI or a direct route.\n4. Direct requests to `POST /api/v1/chat` from unauthorized roles receive HTTP `403` with the expected JSON error.\n5. The backend validates the existing trusted session, token, or supported demo-authentication mechanism.\n6. A frontend-supplied role string alone cannot grant manager permissions.\n7. Other API endpoints do not unintentionally expose manager-only financial or operational data.\n\nPreserve the existing demo-login mechanism. Do not falsely describe demo authentication as production-grade identity verification.\n\nAdd or update targeted tests if any of these checks fail. Do not remove tests or weaken assertions.\n\n# PRIORITY 4 \342\200\224 Verify AI Assistant and Gemini integration\n\nInspect the existing AI Assistant implementation, API client, Express route, authentication middleware, and server configuration.\n\nThe previous HTML/JSON error was:\n\n`Unexpected token \'<\', \"<!doctype \"... is not valid JSON`\n\nThe frontend and backend reportedly have improved response handling. Confirm that the actual application no longer experiences this failure.\n\nTest:\n- Correct request URL and HTTP method.\n- Successful JSON responses with the expected content type.\n- Non-2xx responses.\n- HTML responses and malformed JSON.\n- Missing or invalid Gemini API key.\n- Quota and network failures.\n- Rule-based fallback behaviour.\n- Manager-only authorization.\n\n### Data-grounded answers\n\nTest questions such as:\n- \342\200\234How much idli should be prepared for tomorrow\'s breakfast?\342\200\235\n- \342\200\234Which dishes had the most leftovers this week?\342\200\235\n- \342\200\234How does attendance compare with the previous period?\342\200\235\n- \342\200\234Which preparation targets have been approved?\342\200\235\n- \342\200\234What actions could reduce food waste?\342\200\235\n\nFor preparation recommendations:\n1. Identify the applicable date and meal period.\n2. Check the available menu.\n3. Retrieve the approved preparation target if one exists.\n4. Otherwise calculate an estimate only if the available records support it.\n5. State the quantity, unit, and basis.\n6. Clearly distinguish an estimate from an approved target.\n7. Report insufficient data instead of inventing an answer.\n\nKeep operational calculations grounded in actual application records. Gemini can explain verified results, but must not fabricate records or numerical outcomes.\n\n### API key safety\n\nRead `GEMINI_API_KEY` only from the server-side environment. Never expose it in frontend code, responses, logs, prompts, screenshots, or GitHub.\n\nIf possible, make a real Gemini API request and report whether it succeeds. If the key is unavailable, invalid, or quota-limited, preserve the fallback and state the actual outcome.\n\nDo not enable billing or create cloud resources without explicit approval. Do not claim live Gemini integration works unless a real request succeeds.\n\n# PRIORITY 5 \342\200\224 Verify the complete business workflow\n\nTest the following sequence in the running application:\n\n1. Manager creates or updates a preparation plan.\n2. Manager approves the plan.\n3. The approved target becomes available to Kitchen Staff.\n4. Student/Diner receives the intended notification.\n5. The notification unread count updates correctly.\n6. Opening the notification leads to the appropriate approved menu or screen.\n\nVerify that the UI reflects the underlying application state. Check for stale data, duplicate notifications, broken navigation, and inconsistent role-specific views.\n\nDetermine the actual persistence model. If state is stored only in browser localStorage, say so. Do not claim cross-device synchronization or database persistence without verifying it.\n\n# PRIORITY 6 \342\200\224 Forecasting, analytics, and integration credibility\n\nInspect the current forecasting implementation.\n\nVerify:\n- Historical baselines and trend calculations.\n- Date and meal-period handling.\n- Units and preparation quantities.\n- Safety buffers and rounding.\n- Forecast versus actual comparisons.\n- Waste and leftover calculations.\n- Filters, charts, and summary cards.\n\nEnsure the UI accurately describes the model. If forecasting is deterministic or rule-based, label it accordingly rather than claiming a trained machine-learning model.\n\nDo not fabricate:\n- Profit or loss.\n- Revenue or operating costs.\n- Food-waste reductions.\n- Forecast accuracy or model evaluation metrics.\n- Real institution data or operational results.\n\nCheck whether PostgreSQL/Cloud SQL, n8n, external notifications, and other integrations are genuinely connected. Keep unconfigured integrations clearly labelled as such.\n\n# PRIORITY 7 \342\200\224 Complete visual and interaction polish\n\nReview every page and fix genuine defects, including:\n- Broken image assets or icons.\n- Inconsistent button variants.\n- Misaligned headers and cards.\n- Unreadable chart labels.\n- Tables overflowing small screens.\n- Overlapping dialogs or dropdowns.\n- Missing loading indicators.\n- Dead buttons and nonfunctional links.\n- Misleading empty states.\n- Console errors and failed network requests.\n\nUse existing dependencies and components where practical. Avoid introducing unnecessary libraries, major architectural changes, or risky visual rewrites.\n\nDo not sacrifice usability for decoration. Prioritize clarity, consistent spacing, responsive layouts, and working interactions.\n\n# PRIORITY 8 \342\200\224 Testing and validation\n\nRun the available:\n- TypeScript checks.\n- Production build.\n- Unit tests.\n- Relevant integration tests.\n- API and authorization tests.\n- Forecasting and fallback tests.\n\nThen inspect the actual browser preview.\n\nVerify:\n1. Design A appears on the actual initial route.\n2. Desktop and mobile layouts render correctly.\n3. All three demo roles navigate to the correct experiences.\n4. The AI Assistant is accessible only to Mess Manager.\n5. Backend authorization rejects unauthorized roles.\n6. Manager chat returns valid JSON.\n7. A preparation question uses available records or reports missing data.\n8. Gemini succeeds or fallback behaviour is honestly identified.\n9. Manager approval updates kitchen targets and student notifications.\n10. Existing dashboards, menus, forecasts, and analytics remain functional.\n11. No new runtime errors, broken routes, or failed API requests remain.\n\nIf the application reports runtime errors, inspect their exact details and fix the real cause. A successful build is not sufficient proof of runtime correctness.\n\nIf browser preview, live Gemini calls, or external integrations cannot be tested, explicitly report what remains unverified.\n\n# PRIORITY 9 \342\200\224 Final submission readiness\n\nReview the existing project documentation and submission materials without overwriting useful work.\n\nEnsure:\n- Setup and run instructions match the current project.\n- Demo roles and synthetic data are clearly disclosed.\n- Implemented features are distinguished from planned or unconfigured integrations.\n- Architecture and forecasting descriptions match the actual code.\n- Known limitations are documented.\n- Existing tests and source files remain intact.\n- The project is ready for a stable demonstration.\n\nDo not push commits, publish deployments, or change repository settings unless explicitly authorized.\n\n# FINAL REPORT\n\nProvide a concise, evidence-based report containing:\n\n1. Files changed and the reason for each change.\n2. UI defects found and fixed.\n3. Actual login and dashboard preview verification.\n4. Manager, Kitchen Staff, and Student/Diner test results.\n5. AI Assistant authorization results.\n6. API root cause and verification results.\n7. Gemini live-request or fallback status.\n8. Approval-to-notification workflow results.\n9. Build and test results, including failures or skipped tests.\n10. Actual persistence and integration limitations.\n11. Remaining issues that could affect the hackathon demo.\n\nDo not claim that every feature works merely because the build passes. Distinguish code changes from features genuinely tested in the running application.\n\nStart by inspecting the current state, identifying the highest-impact verified gaps, and fixing them in a safe order. Preserve working features and avoid unnecessary rewrites."
    user_requests: "Fix the errors in the app\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_START]\nfix_it_trigger: manual\nerror 0: [DB] Database configuration error: Missing required environment variables (SQL_HOST, SQL_DB_NAME, SQL_USER, SQL_PASSWORD). Configure these in .env before using database persistence.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_END]"
    user_requests: "Before making any more changes, audit your work against the complete SmartMess AI final audit prompt I previously provided in this conversation.\n\nDo not modify any files yet.\n\nProvide an evidence-based status report for each requirement:\n1. Login Design A: identify the files changed and confirm whether the actual rendered page was inspected.\n2. Manager-only AI Assistant: confirm sidebar visibility, frontend route protection, backend authorization, and tests for unauthorized requests.\n3. Manager, Kitchen Staff, and Student/Diner dashboards: list which pages were actually inspected and tested.\n4. AI chat/API: confirm the request was tested through the running UI, whether valid JSON was returned, and whether a real Gemini API request succeeded or fallback was used.\n5. Approval-to-notification workflow: state whether the complete workflow was executed and verified.\n6. Database/demo mode: explain what changed, whether data persists, and whether the application works without a database.\n7. Responsive UI: state whether desktop and mobile layouts were actually inspected.\n8. Build, TypeScript checks, tests, runtime errors, and failed or skipped tests: provide actual results.\n\nFor each requirement, label it VERIFIED, CODE-IMPLEMENTED BUT NOT TESTED, NOT IMPLEMENTED, or BLOCKED. Distinguish previous work from changes made in the latest run.\n\nUse the existing code and actual test results as evidence. Do not infer success from a successful build. Do not make additional changes during this audit. If a check was not performed, say so honestly."
    user_requests: "# SmartMess AI \342\200\224 Fix the Login Page Visual Mismatch in the Actual Running App\n\nThe previous implementation reports say Design A is complete, but the actual rendered preview still shows the old centered landing page with a large headline, three horizontal feature cards, and three role cards. This is NOT the requested Design A.\n\nMake the actual rendered login page visibly different. Do not merely change colours, spacing, typography, or card styles.\n\n## Required design\n\nCreate a genuine desktop split-screen layout:\n\n**LEFT SIDE \342\200\224 approximately 55% width**\n- Full-height institutional dining hall or modern commercial kitchen photograph.\n- Dark navy overlay for readable text.\n- SmartMess AI logo and brand name at the top.\n- Headline: \342\200\234Smarter Kitchens. Happier Campuses.\342\200\235\n- Short supporting text about intelligent meal-demand forecasting and food-waste prevention.\n- Three compact feature highlights: AI Demand Forecasting, Reduce Food Waste, and Better Dining Experience.\n- Attractive composition using the photo as a visual focal point.\n\n**RIGHT SIDE \342\200\224 approximately 45% width**\n- A distinct, elevated sign-in/demo panel.\n- Heading: \342\200\234Welcome to SmartMess AI\342\200\235.\n- Short instruction to explore the demo.\n- Three compact, clearly differentiated demo-role options:\n  1. Mess Manager\n  2. Kitchen Staff\n  3. Student / Diner\n- Keep the existing functional role-selection actions and navigation.\n- Display \342\200\234Demo access \302\267 Synthetic data\342\200\235.\n\nUse deep navy, emerald accents, polished typography, restrained glows, and consistent spacing. Do not keep the existing centered hero layout or three large role cards below it.\n\n## Responsive behaviour\n- Desktop: side-by-side split-screen layout.\n- Tablet: adapt widths and spacing without overlap.\n- Mobile: stack the photo/story and demo panel vertically, keeping all role choices accessible.\n- No horizontal overflow, clipped text, or broken images.\n\n## Inspect and edit the correct component\n1. Inspect the current login component, route, and CSS/Tailwind classes.\n2. Determine why the previous redesign did not change the rendered layout.\n3. Edit the component actually used by the initial application route. Do not create an unused component or mockup.\n4. Check whether the preview is stale or the wrong route/component is being displayed.\n5. Preserve the existing demo sign-in logic, app state, backend, and other pages.\n\n## Fix the role-access inconsistency\nThe Student/Diner role card currently advertises \342\200\234Ask AI Assistant about menu items & timings\342\200\235. Remove that claim because the conversational AI Assistant is reserved for Mess Manager. Students should instead see wording such as \342\200\234View published menus, meal timings, and notifications\342\200\235.\n\nVerify that:\n- Only Mess Manager sees the AI Assistant navigation item.\n- Kitchen Staff and Student/Diner cannot access the chat through direct navigation or direct API requests.\n- Existing student-facing menus and notifications still work.\n\n## Required proof of completion\nDo not stop after generating code or passing the build.\n\n1. Run the app and open the actual initial route.\n2. Inspect the rendered desktop preview.\n3. Confirm the photograph visibly occupies the left panel and the demo panel occupies the right.\n4. Inspect a mobile-width preview.\n5. Click all three demo-role actions and confirm they still navigate correctly.\n6. Run the relevant build and tests.\n7. Report the files changed and any remaining limitations.\n\nIf the actual preview still displays the centered hero and role cards underneath, the task is NOT complete. Fix the cause and inspect the rendered page again. Do not claim Design A is implemented based on source-code changes alone."
    user_requests: "# SmartMess AI \342\200\224 Art-Directed Login Redesign (Remove the AI-Generated Template Look)\n\nRedesign the ACTUAL initial login/demo-entry page of my existing SmartMess AI application. The current page looks like a generic AI-generated landing page. I want a deliberate, professionally art-directed enterprise product experience.\n\nDo not create a new project. Do not rebuild the entire application. Do not change backend functionality, role permissions, routing, or existing workflows. Focus on the initial page and its styling.\n\n## 1. Design objective\n\nMake the page feel like a real enterprise software product designed by an experienced product designer for university dining operations.\n\nVisual references in spirit:\n- Editorial typography and restrained layouts.\n- High-quality hospitality and institutional dining photography.\n- Enterprise product onboarding.\n- Strong alignment and generous but purposeful whitespace.\n- A clear, practical path into the demo.\n\nThe design must feel credible, calm, precise, and useful. Do not make it look like an AI startup landing-page template.\n\n## 2. Remove these visual patterns from the current page\n\n- Oversized gradient text spanning multiple lines.\n- Multiple glowing gradients over the background photograph.\n- Huge rounded cards repeated for every user role.\n- Long explanatory paragraphs and three-item checklists inside each role card.\n- Excessive pill badges, decorative icons, borders, and shadows.\n- Generic logo tiles containing a single letter as a substitute for proper brand identity.\n- Repeated \342\200\234AI-powered\342\200\235 labels and redundant product messaging.\n- Marketing content that pushes the role-selection controls below the first screen.\n- Unnecessary animation and neon effects.\n\nDo not simply change the colours and retain the same layout. Rethink the composition.\n\n## 3. New layout: compact editorial split screen\n\nUse a clean, balanced two-column layout with a restrained visual style.\n\n### Left side \342\200\224 approximately 52\342\200\22355%\n\nCreate a visually grounded brand/storytelling panel.\n\n- Use one high-quality, realistic photograph of an institutional dining hall, a clean commercial kitchen, or a well-organized meal-preparation area.\n- Use a natural photographic treatment with restrained darkening only where required for legibility.\n- Avoid strong blur, artificial glowing outlines, neon waves, decorative orbital graphics, and multiple overlapping overlays.\n- Place the SmartMess AI wordmark at the top using a simple leaf or food-related mark from the existing icon library.\n- Use a concise eyebrow label: \342\200\234DINING OPERATIONS INTELLIGENCE\342\200\235.\n- Use this headline: \342\200\234Plan better. Prepare smarter. Waste less.\342\200\235\n- Supporting copy: \342\200\234One workspace for meal demand forecasting, kitchen preparation, and food-waste monitoring.\342\200\235\n- At the bottom, include three compact capabilities in a single row or a simple vertical list:\n  \342\200\234Demand forecasting\342\200\235, \342\200\234Preparation planning\342\200\235, \342\200\234Waste monitoring\342\200\235.\n\nThe left panel should communicate the product in seconds, without looking like an advertisement. Keep the content visually balanced and avoid oversized typography.\n\n### Right side \342\200\224 approximately 45\342\200\22348%\n\nThis is the primary interaction area. Users need to enter the demo, not read a sales pitch.\n\nUse a clean, solid background with ample whitespace.\n\nAt the top of the interaction area, show:\n\n**Welcome to SmartMess AI**\n\n\342\200\234Choose your workspace to continue.\342\200\235\n\nThen show the three demo roles as compact, well-designed selectable rows, not large marketing cards.\n\nEach row should contain:\n- A small, consistent icon.\n- Role name.\n- One concise description.\n- A subtle arrow or clear selection indicator.\n\nRoles and descriptions:\n\n1. **Mess Manager** \342\200\224 Forecasts, menu approvals and waste analytics.\n2. **Kitchen Staff** \342\200\224 Preparation targets and serving records.\n3. **Student / Diner** \342\200\224 Approved menus and dining notifications.\n\nMake the entire row clickable, with a clear hover and keyboard-focus state. Maintain good contrast and predictable spacing.\n\nSelecting a role must use the existing demo sign-in flow. Do not invent email/password authentication, accounts, or forms that the application does not support.\n\nOnly the Mess Manager role should be shown as having access to the AI Assistant. Do not advertise the chatbot to students or kitchen staff.\n\nAt the bottom, include a small, unobtrusive note: \342\200\234Demo environment \302\267 Synthetic data\342\200\235.\n\n## 4. Colour, typography and spacing\n\nUse a restrained, intentional palette:\n- Primary background: warm off-white or very light neutral.\n- Text: deep charcoal or deep forest green.\n- Accent: restrained emerald.\n- Secondary surfaces: subtle neutral tones.\n- Borders: thin and low contrast.\n\nA dark forest-green photographic panel may be used on the left, but avoid turning the whole page into a generic dark-neon interface.\n\nUse the project\'s existing typography if it is suitable. Otherwise, choose a clean, highly legible sans-serif with a deliberate hierarchy:\n- Moderate headline size.\n- Clear section heading.\n- Compact descriptions.\n- Consistent body text.\n- No unnecessary uppercase labels everywhere.\n\nUse a consistent spacing scale and align content to a clear grid. Prefer flat surfaces and subtle dividers over nested cards and heavy shadows.\n\n## 5. First-screen usability\n\nAt a typical laptop resolution, the user should see:\n- The brand and complete headline.\n- The concise product explanation.\n- All three role options.\n- The synthetic-data disclosure.\n\nAvoid forcing the user to scroll merely to choose a role. If the viewport is too short, allow sensible scrolling rather than clipping content.\n\nOn mobile:\n- Stack the storytelling and interaction areas.\n- Reduce image height.\n- Keep all three role options easy to find and tap.\n- Prevent horizontal scrolling.\n- Maintain readable text and accessible focus states.\n\n## 6. Preserve existing functionality\n\nBefore editing, inspect the current login component and existing role-selection flow.\n\nPreserve:\n- The existing three demo roles.\n- Correct role-specific navigation.\n- Manager-only AI Assistant restrictions in both frontend and backend.\n- Existing routing and session/token behaviour.\n- All dashboards, forecasting, menu management, kitchen operations, approvals, waste analytics, and notifications.\n- Existing server and API logic.\n\nDo not edit unrelated pages unless required to fix a regression caused by the login redesign.\n\nDo not introduce new dependencies just for visual styling if the existing project can achieve the result.\n\n## 7. Validate the actual result\n\nAfter implementing the redesign:\n\n1. Run the production build and relevant tests.\n2. Launch the actual application preview.\n3. Inspect the rendered initial page at desktop and mobile widths.\n4. Confirm that the oversized gradient headline, repetitive large role cards, and heavy overlays are gone.\n5. Confirm that all three role options are visible and functional.\n6. Click each role and verify correct navigation.\n7. Check for broken images, clipping, overflow, console errors, and broken interactions.\n8. Correct the rendered UI if it still looks like a generic AI-generated template.\n\nDo not stop after editing the JSX or CSS. Do not report success based on a successful build alone.\n\nAt the end, briefly explain the changes and report whether the actual preview was inspected.\n\nStart by inspecting the existing login component, then implement this new art direction directly in the existing page."
    user_requests: "Fix the errors in the app\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_START]\nfix_it_trigger: manual\nerror 0: [Chat] Gemini API call skipped/failed, falling back to deterministic data assistant: {\"error\":{\"code\":400,\"message\":\"API key not valid. Please pass a valid API key.\",\"status\":\"INVALID_ARGUMENT\",\"details\":[{\"@type\":\"type.googleapis.com/google.rpc.ErrorInfo\",\"reason\":\"API_KEY_INVALID\",\"domain\":\"googleapis.com\",\"metadata\":{\"service\":\"generativelanguage.googleapis.com\"}},{\"@type\":\"type.googleapis.com/google.rpc.LocalizedMessage\",\"locale\":\"en-US\",\"message\":\"API key not valid. Please pass a valid API key.\"}]}}\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_END]"
    user_requests: "Make a focused final refinement to the existing SmartMess AI login page. Preserve the current split-screen Design A, all three demo-role options, existing navigation, and all working application features. Do not rebuild the page from scratch.\n\n1. Keep the premium navy/emerald design and dining-hall image.\n2. Preserve the three demo-role entry points for Mess Manager, Kitchen Staff, and Student/Diner. Make their labels and actions clearly communicate that they enter a synthetic-data demo.\n3. Do not add nonfunctional email/password fields. If real authentication is not implemented, keep the demo-role entry flow honest and clear.\n4. Verify the existing session behaviour. If a valid session is intentionally persisted, document how it works. Do not automatically bypass authentication for a real account-based flow, and do not trust a client-side role selection as production-grade authentication.\n5. Replace unsupported claims such as \342\200\234Verified operations runtime\342\200\235 and \342\200\234Enterprise institutional tier\342\200\235 with accurate product messaging unless those claims can be verified.\n6. Improve small-screen responsiveness, keyboard accessibility, focus states, contrast, and card hover/pressed states without adding distracting animation.\n7. Verify that selecting each demo role opens the correct existing dashboard and that role permissions remain enforced by the backend.\n8. Inspect the actual rendered desktop and mobile previews. Run the build and relevant tests, and report any remaining limitations honestly.\n\nMake only changes that are necessary. Preserve the current working login page and do not modify unrelated features."
    user_requests: "Continue working on the existing SmartMess AI project from its current saved state. The previous generation was cancelled with \342\200\234The caller does not have permission\342\200\235 and an unexpected error.\n\nBefore making any changes:\n1. Inspect the current project state and identify any incomplete or partially applied edits.\n2. Check whether the existing app still runs and whether the latest login redesign and manager-only AI access restrictions are present.\n3. Identify the exact remaining runtime or build errors, if any.\n4. Do not reset the project, rewrite working files, or remove features.\n5. Report the current state and the safest next action before attempting substantial edits.\n\nDo not expose secrets or change project permissions, billing, or cloud resources."
    user_requests: "# SMARTMESS AI \342\200\224 FINAL APPLICATION AUDIT, UI POLISH & HACKATHON READINESS\n\nYou are continuing work on my existing SmartMess AI project. The latest implementation reportedly includes the Design A login page, manager-only AI Assistant access, server-side role authorization, improved JSON handling, and AI fallback behaviour.\n\nYour task is to conduct a comprehensive final audit of the ENTIRE application, fix remaining real issues, improve visual consistency, and verify the running app before declaring completion.\n\n## NON-NEGOTIABLE RULES\n\n1. Do not create a new project or replace the current application.\n2. Do not reset the repository or discard working changes.\n3. Inspect the current code and rendered application before editing.\n4. Preserve all working functionality, existing API contracts, routes, and tests.\n5. Do not rewrite components merely to demonstrate activity.\n6. Fix verified problems first; make visual improvements only where they add real value.\n7. Never claim a feature is verified unless it was actually tested.\n8. Do not expose API keys, secrets, private records, or sensitive server errors.\n9. If the environment cannot perform a test, report that limitation honestly.\n\n## PRIORITY 1 \342\200\224 Audit the actual UI across the entire application\n\nInspect the actual rendered application, including every existing page and dashboard used by each role.\n\n### Global design system\n\nMaintain a consistent visual identity:\n- Deep navy: `#07111F`\n- Elevated panels: `#0D1B2A`\n- Emerald accent: `#10D9A0`\n- High-contrast white primary text.\n- Muted blue-grey secondary text.\n- Consistent typography, spacing, radii, borders, shadows, icons, and button styles.\n\nMake the product look like a cohesive, premium AI SaaS platform, not a collection of unrelated templates.\n\nAudit:\n- Navigation and active states.\n- Page headings and content hierarchy.\n- Cards, tables, charts, forms, dialogs, dropdowns, and notifications.\n- Alignment, whitespace, overflow, and scrolling.\n- Loading, empty, error, and success states.\n- Keyboard navigation, focus indicators, contrast, and touch targets.\n- Responsive behaviour at desktop, tablet, and mobile widths.\n\nRemove redundant decoration and visual clutter. Do not add fake data, meaningless charts, fake testimonials, fake performance statistics, or buttons that do nothing.\n\n### Login page \342\200\224 Design A\n\nVerify that the actual initial route shows the requested split-screen experience:\n- Left: SmartMess AI branding, institutional dining imagery, product messaging, and three feature highlights.\n- Right: welcome panel and clear demo-role selection for Mess Manager, Kitchen Staff, and Student/Diner.\n- Responsive desktop split layout and mobile stacking.\n- Clear disclosure that demo access uses synthetic data.\n\nIf Design A is already correct, preserve it. If it is incomplete or the old layout still appears, fix the actual login route and inspect the rendered preview again.\n\n## PRIORITY 2 \342\200\224 Audit each role independently\n\n### A. Mess Manager\n\nVerify:\n- Manager dashboard and summary metrics.\n- Demand forecasts and preparation recommendations.\n- Menu planning and management.\n- Plan review and approval.\n- Waste and leftover analytics.\n- Relevant operational trends.\n- Manager-only AI Assistant.\n- Useful error, loading, and empty states.\n\nEnsure metrics correspond to the records and filters shown. Clearly distinguish forecasts, estimates, approved quantities, historical observations, and synthetic demo values.\n\nNever invent financial figures or claim actual cost savings or waste reduction without evidence.\n\n### B. Kitchen Staff\n\nVerify:\n- Correct role-specific dashboard and navigation.\n- Visibility of approved preparation targets.\n- Correct menu, meal-period, and preparation information.\n- Existing preparation and serving logs.\n- Operational feedback after successful or failed actions.\n- Updates following manager approval.\n\nKitchen Staff must not access the manager-only AI Assistant or private institutional analytics.\n\n### C. Student/Diner\n\nVerify:\n- Approved menu visibility.\n- Meal timings and dining information.\n- Relevant notifications and unread counts.\n- Correct notification navigation.\n- Clear empty states when a menu is unavailable.\n- Responsive layout.\n\nStudents must not access manager-only chat, institutional profit/loss, other students\' private records, or internal kitchen analytics.\n\n## PRIORITY 3 \342\200\224 Verify authorization and security\n\nThe latest changes reportedly restrict the AI Assistant to Mess Manager through the sidebar, frontend routes, and backend authorization.\n\nVerify all three layers rather than assuming they work.\n\n1. Mess Manager can access the AI Assistant.\n2. Kitchen Staff cannot access it through the UI or a direct route.\n3. Student/Diner cannot access it through the UI or a direct route.\n4. Direct requests to `POST /api/v1/chat` from unauthorized roles receive HTTP `403` with the expected JSON error.\n5. The backend validates the existing trusted session, token, or supported demo-authentication mechanism.\n6. A frontend-supplied role string alone cannot grant manager permissions.\n7. Other API endpoints do not unintentionally expose manager-only financial or operational data.\n\nPreserve the existing demo-login mechanism. Do not falsely describe demo authentication as production-grade identity verification.\n\nAdd or update targeted tests if any of these checks fail. Do not remove tests or weaken assertions.\n\n## PRIORITY 4 \342\200\224 Verify AI Assistant and Gemini integration\n\nInspect the existing AI Assistant implementation, API client, Express route, authentication middleware, and server configuration.\n\nThe previous HTML/JSON error was:\n\n`Unexpected token \'<\', \"<!doctype \"... is not valid JSON`\n\nThe frontend and backend reportedly have improved response handling. Confirm that the actual application no longer experiences this failure.\n\nTest:\n- Correct request URL and HTTP method.\n- Successful JSON responses with the expected content type.\n- Non-2xx responses.\n- HTML responses and malformed JSON.\n- Missing or invalid Gemini API key.\n- Quota and network failures.\n- Rule-based fallback behaviour.\n- Manager-only authorization.\n\n### Data-grounded answers\n\nTest questions such as:\n- \342\200\234How much idli should be prepared for tomorrow\'s breakfast?\342\200\235\n- \342\200\234Which dishes had the most leftovers this week?\342\200\235\n- \342\200\234How does attendance compare with the previous period?\342\200\235\n- \342\200\234Which preparation targets have been approved?\342\200\235\n- \342\200\234What actions could reduce food waste?\342\200\235\n\nFor preparation recommendations:\n1. Identify the applicable date and meal period.\n2. Check the available menu.\n3. Retrieve the approved preparation target if one exists.\n4. Otherwise calculate an estimate only if the available records support it.\n5. State the quantity, unit, and basis.\n6. Clearly distinguish an estimate from an approved target.\n7. Report insufficient data instead of inventing an answer.\n\nKeep operational calculations grounded in actual application records. Gemini can explain verified results, but must not fabricate records or numerical outcomes.\n\n### API key safety\n\nRead `GEMINI_API_KEY` only from the server-side environment. Never expose it in frontend code, responses, logs, prompts, screenshots, or GitHub.\n\nIf possible, make a real Gemini API request and report whether it succeeds. If the key is unavailable, invalid, or quota-limited, preserve the fallback and state the actual outcome.\n\nDo not enable billing or create cloud resources without explicit approval. Do not claim live Gemini integration works unless a real request succeeds.\n\n## PRIORITY 5 \342\200\224 Verify the complete business workflow\n\nTest the following sequence in the running application:\n\n1. Manager creates or updates a preparation plan.\n2. Manager approves the plan.\n3. The approved target becomes available to Kitchen Staff.\n4. Student/Diner receives the intended notification.\n5. The notification unread count updates correctly.\n6. Opening the notification leads to the appropriate approved menu or screen.\n\nVerify that the UI reflects the underlying application state. Check for stale data, duplicate notifications, broken navigation, and inconsistent role-specific views.\n\nDetermine the actual persistence model. If state is stored only in browser localStorage, say so. Do not claim cross-device synchronization or database persistence without verifying it.\n\n## PRIORITY 6 \342\200\224 Forecasting, analytics, and integration credibility\n\nInspect the current forecasting implementation.\n\nVerify:\n- Historical baselines and trend calculations.\n- Date and meal-period handling.\n- Units and preparation quantities.\n- Safety buffers and rounding.\n- Forecast versus actual comparisons.\n- Waste and leftover calculations.\n- Filters, charts, and summary cards.\n\nEnsure the UI accurately describes the model. If forecasting is deterministic or rule-based, label it accordingly rather than claiming a trained machine-learning model.\n\nDo not fabricate:\n- Profit or loss.\n- Revenue or operating costs.\n- Food-waste reductions.\n- Forecast accuracy or model evaluation metrics.\n- Real institution data or operational results.\n\nCheck whether PostgreSQL/Cloud SQL, n8n, external notifications, and other integrations are genuinely connected. Keep unconfigured integrations clearly labelled as such.\n\n## PRIORITY 7 \342\200\224 Complete visual and interaction polish\n\nReview every page and fix genuine defects, including:\n- Broken image assets or icons.\n- Inconsistent button variants.\n- Misaligned headers and cards.\n- Unreadable chart labels.\n- Tables overflowing small screens.\n- Overlapping dialogs or dropdowns.\n- Missing loading indicators.\n- Dead buttons and nonfunctional links.\n- Misleading empty states.\n- Console errors and failed network requests.\n\nUse existing dependencies and components where practical. Avoid introducing unnecessary libraries, major architectural changes, or risky visual rewrites.\n\nDo not sacrifice usability for decoration. Prioritize clarity, consistent spacing, responsive layouts, and working interactions.\n\n## PRIORITY 8 \342\200\224 Testing and validation\n\nRun the available:\n- TypeScript checks.\n- Production build.\n- Unit tests.\n- Relevant integration tests.\n- API and authorization tests.\n- Forecasting and fallback tests.\n\nThen inspect the actual browser preview.\n\nVerify:\n1. Design A appears on the actual initial route.\n2. Desktop and mobile layouts render correctly.\n3. All three demo roles navigate to the correct experiences.\n4. The AI Assistant is accessible only to Mess Manager.\n5. Backend authorization rejects unauthorized roles.\n6. Manager chat returns valid JSON.\n7. A preparation question uses available records or reports missing data.\n8. Gemini succeeds or fallback behaviour is honestly identified.\n9. Manager approval updates kitchen targets and student notifications.\n10. Existing dashboards, menus, forecasts, and analytics remain functional.\n11. No new runtime errors, broken routes, or failed API requests remain.\n\nIf the application reports runtime errors, inspect their exact details and fix the real cause. A successful build is not sufficient proof of runtime correctness.\n\nIf browser preview, live Gemini calls, or external integrations cannot be tested, explicitly report what remains unverified.\n\n## PRIORITY 9 \342\200\224 Final submission readiness\n\nReview the existing project documentation and submission materials without overwriting useful work.\n\nEnsure:\n- Setup and run instructions match the current project.\n- Demo roles and synthetic data are clearly disclosed.\n- Implemented features are distinguished from planned or unconfigured integrations.\n- Architecture and forecasting descriptions match the actual code.\n- Known limitations are documented.\n- Existing tests and source files remain intact.\n- The project is ready for a stable demonstration.\n\nDo not push commits, publish deployments, or change repository settings unless explicitly authorized.\n\n## FINAL REPORT\n\nProvide a concise, evidence-based report containing:\n\n1. Files changed and the reason for each change.\n2. UI defects found and fixed.\n3. Actual login and dashboard preview verification.\n4. Manager, Kitchen Staff, and Student/Diner test results.\n5. AI Assistant authorization results.\n6. API root cause and verification results.\n7. Gemini live-request or fallback status.\n8. Approval-to-notification workflow results.\n9. Build and test results, including failures or skipped tests.\n10. Actual persistence and integration limitations.\n11. Remaining issues that could affect the hackathon demo.\n\nDo not claim that every feature works merely because the build passes. Distinguish code changes from features genuinely tested in the running application.\n\nStart by inspecting the current state, identifying the highest-impact verified gaps, and fixing them in a safe order. Preserve working features and avoid unnecessary rewrites."
    render_latest_checkpoint: true
    total_subagent_count: 0
  }
}
