ties back to the reported \"Failed query\" message.\n\n\n**Uncovering the Error Propagation**\n\nI\'ve just made a critical connection: Drizzle\'s `db.select()` call results in `pool.query()` which directly rejects with a `DatabaseConfigurationError`. Drizzle then catches this and repackages it as a \"Failed query\" error, with the original configuration error as the cause. So, the issue isn\'t within Drizzle, but in the underlying pool configuration! I\'m now understanding how the `DatabaseConfigurationError` propagates up through the stack to generate the specific error message. I see the flow from `db.select()` -> `pool.query()` -> Error wrapping! I\'m moving on to understanding why the database was seeded at all!\n\n\n**Clarifying Conditional Logic**\n\nI\'m now tracing the execution of the seeding function. I\'m focusing on the condition where seeding is attempted only if tables are empty. I\'m examining if the status configuration could change after `resolveDatabaseConfig` ran but before seeding, and what the source of the \"[Server] Seed check deferred or non-fatal\" log is. I\'m investigating how the configuration is defined and whether env variables have been set. I\'m checking the specific use of `SQL_HOST`, `SQL_DB_NAME`, `SQL_USER`, `SQL_PASSWORD`, `SQL_ADMIN_USER`, and `SQL_ADMIN_PASSWORD`.\n\n\n"
