 the record insertion process. I see I\'m handling the various properties, like `serviceDate`, `meal`, `status`, and diner counts. I\'m focusing on how I\'m handling the null and undefined values, specifically for the diner counts. Also, I\'m examining how `r.items` is handled, especially the item-specific data and how I\'m parsing the data from the frontend.\n\n\n**Refining Data Handling**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the data sanitization within the record creation. I\'ve focused on `POST /records`, and specifically the parsing logic for `it.suggestedPrep`, `it.approvedPrep`, `it.prepared`, and `it.consumed`. I realized I need to be explicit about handling null/undefined values within each of these properties. I\'m also ensuring that these values are being converted to numbers. Also, the fallback mechanism I implemented returns the request body if the database is unconfigured.\n\n\n**Implementing Role-Based Access**\n\nI\'m now integrating role-based access controls to the record update endpoint. I\'ve added checks to ensure only managers can approve preparation plans, and kitchen staff or managers can modify kitchen plans. I\'m also addressing the database configuration check, and the database select if it exists. Finally, I\'ve added a check for the existence of the updated record before continuing.\n\n\n**Enhancing Status Updates**\n\nI\'m now implementing logic to update a meal record\'s status, actual diners, approval, and completion timestamps. I\'ve ensured only authorized roles can make changes. I\'m checking for updates to `actualDiners` as well, ensuring I am accounting for the existing data. I\'m also addressing historical records. Finally, I\'m ensuring any required information gets updated accordingly.\n\n\n\n**Refining Item Updates**\n\nI\'m now refining the item updates logic within the record update route, specifically `PUT /records/:id`. I\'m focusing on the conditional updates of item properties, ensuring I\'m explicitly handling `approvedPrep`, `pr